WEBVTT

00:00.610 --> 00:04.790
We've got a scale here, and as
you see, the scale is balanced.

00:04.790 --> 00:07.550
And we have a
question to answer.

00:07.550 --> 00:09.720
We have this mystery
mass over here.

00:09.720 --> 00:13.560
It's a big question mark on
this blue mass right over here.

00:13.560 --> 00:16.170
And we also have
a bunch of 1-- I

00:16.170 --> 00:18.410
guess we could call
them 1-kilogram masses.

00:18.410 --> 00:21.680
So these are all each
a 1-kilogram mass.

00:21.680 --> 00:24.120
And my question to
you is, what could we

00:24.120 --> 00:27.450
do to either side of
this scale in order

00:27.450 --> 00:29.475
to figure out what
the mystery mass is?

00:29.475 --> 00:31.100
Or maybe we can't
figure it out at all.

00:31.100 --> 00:34.420
Is there something that we can
do, either removing or adding

00:34.420 --> 00:36.420
these things, so that
we can figure out

00:36.420 --> 00:38.190
what this mystery mass is?

00:38.190 --> 00:40.990
And I'll give you a couple of
seconds to think about that.

00:43.990 --> 00:47.210
Well, to figure out what
this mystery mass is,

00:47.210 --> 00:51.200
we essentially just want this
on one side of this scale.

00:51.200 --> 00:52.680
But that by itself isn't enough.

00:52.680 --> 00:55.190
We could just remove these
3, but that won't do the job,

00:55.190 --> 00:57.450
because if we just
remove these 3,

00:57.450 --> 00:59.260
then the left-hand
side of the scale

00:59.260 --> 01:02.650
is clearly going to have less
mass, and it's going to go up,

01:02.650 --> 01:04.390
and the right side
is going to go down.

01:04.390 --> 01:06.431
And that's not going to
give as much information.

01:06.431 --> 01:09.620
It's just going to tell us that
this blue thing has a lower

01:09.620 --> 01:11.760
mass than what's over here.

01:11.760 --> 01:13.870
So just removing this
won't help us much.

01:13.870 --> 01:16.719
It won't let us know that
this is equal to that.

01:16.719 --> 01:19.010
Well, what we've got to do
if we want to keep the scale

01:19.010 --> 01:22.180
balanced is we've got to
remove the same amount of mass

01:22.180 --> 01:24.530
from both sides of the scale.

01:24.530 --> 01:27.070
So if we want to remove
3 things here-- so let

01:27.070 --> 01:30.250
me try my best to
remove 3 things here.

01:30.250 --> 01:33.434
If we want to remove 3--
let me do it like this.

01:33.434 --> 01:34.350
I'll just color on it.

01:34.350 --> 01:35.540
I'll just erase it.

01:35.540 --> 01:37.850
So if we want to
remove 3 things there,

01:37.850 --> 01:41.780
if we did this by itself,
just removed these 3 things,

01:41.780 --> 01:44.340
then the two sides would not
have an equal mass anymore.

01:44.340 --> 01:46.270
This side over here
would have a lower mass.

01:46.270 --> 01:48.495
So we've got to remove
3 from both sides.

01:48.495 --> 01:49.870
So if we really
want to make sure

01:49.870 --> 01:51.244
that our scale is
balanced, we've

01:51.244 --> 01:54.160
got to remove 3 from both sides.

01:54.160 --> 01:57.220
And so if we started off
with the scales balanced

01:57.220 --> 01:59.480
and then we removed
3 from both sides,

01:59.480 --> 02:01.360
the scale will
still be balanced.

02:01.360 --> 02:03.360
And then when we do that,
we have a clearer idea

02:03.360 --> 02:05.900
of what the mass of
this object actually is.

02:05.900 --> 02:07.860
Now, when we remove
3 from both sides,

02:07.860 --> 02:10.500
the scale will
still be balanced.

02:10.500 --> 02:12.600
And we know that this mass
is equal to whatever's

02:12.600 --> 02:13.530
left over here.

02:13.530 --> 02:17.784
It's equal to 1,
2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.

02:17.784 --> 02:19.450
And if we're assuming
they're kilograms,

02:19.450 --> 02:21.410
we'll know that
the question mark

02:21.410 --> 02:26.520
mass is equal to 7 kilograms,
that this right over here

02:26.520 --> 02:30.920
is a 7-kilogram mass.
